From 5abf69e0b02854172dd40b55d16c0f716c450001 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Sebastian Werner <swernerx@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 15 Sep 2026 23:34:19 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] perf(renderer): cut escape runs out of the byte slice
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Both escapers scan `s.as_bytes()` and then hand `&s[start..i]` to the run
copier. That slicing is `str` indexing, so it calls `is_char_boundary` on
both ends of every run — re-establishing at run time exactly what the
scanner has just proven, because a run is always cut at a byte the
classifier flagged and every flagged byte is ASCII. A time profile of the
render stage attributes a visible share of self time to those checks: on
a URL-dense document they are the second largest item after the URL
scanner itself, and they show up on ordinary documentation pages too,
since the check lands once per run and runs are short.

Take the runs from the byte slice instead and copy them as bytes.
`push_run`/`push_run_long` now take `&[u8]` and are `unsafe`, because a
byte slice no longer carries the "whole characters" guarantee a `&str`
did; each escape loop states in one `SAFETY` comment why its cuts fall on
char boundaries — `start` is zero, one past a flagged ASCII byte, or the
end of a percent-encoded run, and `i` is a flagged ASCII byte — and why
the output keeps ending on one. The copy strategies, the scanners and the
growth checks are untouched; `push_run_long`'s long-run case now copies
through `memcpy` directly rather than via `push_str`, which is what
`push_str` did with the reserve already taken.

Output is unchanged. The differential tests gain an independent text
oracle, a UTF-8 re-validation of every escaper result (a run that split a
multi-byte character would leave invalid UTF-8 that string comparison
alone would not catch), a sweep of two-, three-, four-byte and mixed-width
fillers across all three copy widths, and a URL run-length sweep across
the same boundaries.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>
